"Displays nested stage counts to compare remaining quantity and drop-off through a defined sequence.\n\nUse when: Every later stage is a subset of the preceding stage. Readers need absolute counts and conversion rates between stages.\n\nAvoid when: Stages represent unrelated populations or can increase without an explained cohort rule.\n\nVisual grammar: Stage width or bar length encodes the remaining count. Ordered position identifies the stage, while annotations encode stage and overall conversion rates.\n\nBuild: Define one cohort, one observation window, and ordered nested stage criteria. Count qualifying units at each stage using consistent identities. Draw separate centered bars or a clear stepped profile and label counts and denominators.\n\nCheck: Check counts do not rise unless reentry is explicitly part of the definition. Verify each conversion rate names its numerator and denominator. Confirm duplicate units do not inflate stage totals.\n\nPitfalls: A smooth trapezoid can make area imply magnitude even when only width is valid. Prefer aligned zero-based bars for precise drop-off comparisons and keep stage labels outside shapes.\n\nExample: Track synthetic registrations through activation and renewal, labeling count, prior-stage conversion, and overall retention at each stage.\n\nAlternatives: Bar chart, Waterfall chart, Sankey diagram."
